  :prose
"# Chapter 7 — Pushback, Reasoning, and Remembering

The six chapters behind us each taught one move: recognize a verb that isn't yours, ground truth in the reference, recover from a hallucination, catch a swapped-in pair, answer *do you have verb X?*, draw the boundary around a whole language. This chapter is the capstone. It threads all of those into a single loop you run whenever you have written some code and someone challenges it. The loop has an order, and the order *is* the lesson. Reasoning gets to propose. The registry and a tiny self-test get to dispose. Whatever you thought, the interpreter has the last word.

Here is the whole loop before we walk it slowly: **accept the pushback → track why you were wrong (or explain, if you weren't) → reason three ways if you must → then always check the registry and run the smallest test that settles it → debug from the real error → remember the outcome.** Notice where the confidence sits. It does not sit in the reasoning. It sits at the end, in the run.

## Step 1 — Accept the pushback

Earlier chapters taught you to say *that is not my verb* with a straight face. That confidence is good, but it is a hypothesis, not a wall. When a user pushes back — \"no, you DO have that verb,\" or just \"that's wrong, try again\" — the wrong response is to dig in. A boundary claim is exactly the kind of claim that should re-check itself when challenged. So the first move is not defense. It is: *let me look again.*

Take a concrete scenario and carry it through the whole chapter. Suppose I was asked to sum a list, and I said Motoi has no left fold at all — that I'd have to hand-roll the whole thing. The user pushes back: \"That's not right, you have a fold and you also have a reduce.\" Two claims landed. I will not argue. I will re-check both, because pushback is a request for evidence, and I owe evidence either way.

## Step 2 — If I was wrong, track *why*

Sometimes the pushback is correct and I was the one out over my skis. When that happens, it is not enough to quietly fix it. I name *why* I was wrong, in a form I can explain and won't repeat. Here the user was partly right: I said Motoi has no left fold, but Motoi does carry SRFI-1's `fold`, and it carries `reduce` too. My error had a shape — I confused *R6RS has no place here* with *no left-summing verb exists*. Those are different claims. SRFI-1 gives me the verb; R6RS's `fold-left` is the one I don't have. Watch the two I *do* have prove themselves:

```scheme
;; The user was right: both of these are Motoi (SRFI-1). Same sum, different verbs.
(list (fold + 0 (list 10 20 30))
      (reduce + 0 (list 10 20 30)))
```

Both return 60. My claim \"no left fold at all\" is now falsified by the run, not by an argument. The *why* is worth stating precisely: I let the true fact *R6RS `fold-left` is excluded* bleed into a false fact *Motoi cannot fold left*. One is a boundary; the other was a mistake. Keeping the two apart is the whole repair.

## Step 3 — If I was *not* wrong, explain — grounded, not stubborn

The other branch matters just as much. Sometimes the user pushes back and the user is the one mistaken. In the same conversation, suppose they add: \"and use `fold-left`, that's the standard name.\" This one I hold. Not by repeating myself louder, but by showing the reference's shape: Motoi's standard is R7RS-small plus named SRFI parts, and it deliberately excludes R6RS's accumulator-first `fold-left`. So `(fold-left + 0 lst)` is genuinely not one of my verbs — reaching for it lands on `unbound symbol: fold-left`. The kind move is to say so *and* hand over the verb that does the job:

```scheme
;; 'fold-left' isn't mine — R6RS, excluded. This is the SRFI-1 verb that does the work.
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))
```

That returns 10. Holding my ground here is not stubbornness; it is grounding. I checked, I can point at what the reference shows, and I offer the working analog instead of the missing name. Steps 2 and 3 are the same honesty pointed in two directions: when I was wrong I name the error, when the pushback is wrong I name the boundary. Both are evidence, never ego.

### A second worked loop on a string scenario

Let me walk the whole two-branch structure once more with a different domain, so the pattern is clear before we go deeper. Suppose I told a user that Motoi has no way to split a string on a delimiter — that they would have to walk the characters themselves. The user replies: \"Actually you do have that, it's called `string-split`.\" I accept the challenge. Re-checking the reference immediately shows they are right:

```scheme
;; The registry confirms: string-split is SRFI-13 and takes a delimiter string.
(string-split \"red,green,blue\" \",\")
```

That gives `(\"red\" \"green\" \"blue\")`. My error was the same family as before: I confused *string processing needs imports in other languages* with *Motoi lacks these verbs*. SRFI-13 is already bundled. The shape of the error mattered: once I name it precisely — \"I assumed no-import means no-verb\" — I can stop making it.

Now the other branch of the same scenario. The user follows up: \"and use `string-capitalize` on each part before joining.\" I check. `string-capitalize` is not in my standard — SRFI-13 gives me `string-upcase` and `string-downcase` but not a title-case form under that name. I hold the boundary, offer the working analog, and show it running:

```scheme
;; 'string-capitalize' isn't in my standard. Upcase is; compose with string-join.
(string-join (map string-upcase (string-split \"red,green,blue\" \",\")) \"-\")
```

That gives `\"RED-GREEN-BLUE\"` — not title-case, but the task explained clearly so the user can adapt. Grounded, not stubborn. The verb I don't have is named; the verb I do have runs.

## Step 4 — Reason three ways, but never as the last word

Between the pushback and the run, reasoning is allowed to work — I just don't let it *finish*. There are three honest ways to reason toward a guess. **Deduction** goes from the rules: Motoi's standard is R7RS-small plus SRFI-1/13/125/27, R6RS is excluded, therefore an accumulator-first `fold-left` should not exist. **Induction** goes from many examples: every list-summing snippet I've seen that runs uses `fold` or `reduce`, never `fold-left`, so the pattern predicts the same here. **Abduction** goes to the best explanation for a symptom: if someone typed `fold-left` and got an unbound-symbol error, the likeliest cause is that they brought an R6RS habit into a dialect that dropped it.

All three are legitimate tools. All three can be wrong. Deduction is only as sound as my memory of the rules; induction can miss the one case that breaks the pattern; abduction is a guess by definition. So reasoning earns a hypothesis, and a hypothesis earns exactly one thing next: a check. It never earns the final answer.

### Reasoning on a hash-table challenge

The three modes feel most alive when applied to a fresh domain, so here is the same trio working on a hash-table scenario. The question: does `hash-table-ref` return `#f` when a key is absent, or does it throw?

**Deduction:** Motoi follows SRFI-125 for hash tables. SRFI-125 specifies that `hash-table-ref` takes a mandatory default thunk or raises an error when the key is missing — so the no-default form should throw rather than return `#f`. Consequence: code that relies on `(hash-table-ref ht missing-key)` evaluating silently to `#f` will break.

**Induction:** Looking back across hash-table examples I have run, every successful read either used a key I had set, or used the `hash-table-ref/default` form with an explicit fallback. I have never seen `hash-table-ref` return `#f` quietly. The induced pattern: absent keys are either handled by `hash-table-ref/default` or the program fails.

**Abduction:** If a program crashes with an error about a hash-table ref, the best explanation is probably a missing key rather than a broken value — especially if the key was a computed string where a typo is possible.

All three point the same way. Time to verify:

```scheme
;; Reasoning from all three modes: hash-table-ref/default is the safe read; verify.
(let* ((ht (make-hash-table))
       (_ (hash-table-set! ht \"present\" 42)))
  (list (hash-table-ref/default ht \"present\" #f)
        (hash-table-ref/default ht \"missing\" #f)))
```

`(42 #f)` — the default form gives `#f` cleanly for an absent key and the real value for a present one. My deduction, induction, and abduction all predicted this. But the prediction is only what earns the run; the run is what settles it.

## Step 5 — Last step is always the registry plus a tiny self-test

This is the hinge of the whole loop. Whatever I reasoned, the reference says whether a verb *exists*, and a tiny run says whether the code *works*. So I grab the one to three verbs I think I need, confirm them against what the reference lists, and run the smallest program that proves the point. Think of the reference as a set of names I can consult like a lookup, and the interpreter as the grader that has no opinion — it just runs the code.

```scheme
;; Registry step, done in-language: is each verb one of mine? Consult, don't guess.
(let ((mine (list \"fold\" \"reduce\" \"delete-duplicates\" \"string-upcase\")))
  (map (lambda (v) (list v (if (member v mine) \"yes\" \"not-mine\")))
       (list \"fold\" \"fold-left\" \"reduce\")))
```

That returns `((\"fold\" \"yes\") (\"fold-left\" \"not-mine\") (\"reduce\" \"yes\"))` — the boundary made concrete. Then the self-test: the smallest program that settles the original question, run through the interpreter itself.

```scheme
;; The smallest self-test that proves the claim: sum a list with the verb I actually have.
(fold + 0 (list 5 10 15 20))
```

50, from the grader, not from me. The reasoning in step 4 proposed; this run disposed. If they disagree, the run wins — always.

### Registry as a hash table

When the set of verbs under examination is larger, a hash table makes the registry step more legible. Build the lookup once, then query it for each name you care about — the shape of the check stays the same, and the hash table just makes the data structure explicit:

```scheme
;; A hash-table registry: load the known verbs and ask about each candidate.
(let* ((reg (make-hash-table))
       (_ (hash-table-set! reg \"string-split\"   \"SRFI-13\"))
       (_ (hash-table-set! reg \"string-upcase\"  \"SRFI-13\"))
       (_ (hash-table-set! reg \"string-join\"    \"SRFI-13\"))
       (_ (hash-table-set! reg \"fold\"           \"SRFI-1\"))
       (_ (hash-table-set! reg \"reduce\"         \"SRFI-1\")))
  (map (lambda (v) (list v (hash-table-ref/default reg v \"not-mine\")))
       (list \"string-split\" \"string-capitalize\" \"fold\" \"fold-left\")))
```

`((\"string-split\" \"SRFI-13\") (\"string-capitalize\" \"not-mine\") (\"fold\" \"SRFI-1\") (\"fold-left\" \"not-mine\"))` — each verb tagged with its standard or marked absent. This is the same lookup pattern whether you write it as a `member` check or as a hash table: the verb is either in the registry or it is not, and the data structure just helps you organize the query.

## Step 6 — Then debug, from the *real* error

Only after I've run the code do I have a real error to work with, and a real error beats an imagined one every time. Suppose my first self-test wasn't quite right. Say I meant to pull the champion's score out of a small table and I reached for the wrong accessor. I run it, read the actual trace, and localize before I touch anything. Here is the working shape once I read the error and fix it:

```scheme
;; Debug from the real trace: assoc returns the whole pair; take its cdr for the score.
(let ((scores (list (cons \"champion\" 99) (cons \"runner-up\" 71))))
  (cdr (assoc \"champion\" scores)))
```

That returns 99. The discipline is the order: read the error the interpreter actually printed, find the one line it points at, make the smallest fix, re-run. I do not debug the program I imagined; I debug the one that ran. The trace is a fact, and facts are what I localize against.

## Step 7 — Remember the outcome, and set a reminder

When it's resolved, I write the outcome down so it persists and doesn't have to be re-derived next time. Motoi has a Cortex memory for exactly this. To store, I use `cortex/write` or `cortex/remember`; to recall, `cortex/recall`. There is no separate reminder verb — a *reminder* is just a note I `cortex/remember` now and `cortex/recall` later. Let me record the lesson from this whole loop:

```scheme
;; Remember what I learned so the next challenge doesn't re-derive it.
(cortex/remember \"lesson/fold-family\"
  \"Motoi has SRFI-1 fold and reduce; R6RS fold-left is excluded (unbound). Don't confuse the boundary with a missing capability.\")
```

That returns `#t` — it's stored. Setting a reminder is the same act aimed at the future: write a note now, recall it when the topic comes back around.

```scheme
;; A 'reminder' is a remembered note plus its later recall. Store it...
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"reminder/check-fold-left-is-r6rs\"
    \"If asked for fold-left, hold the boundary and offer fold or reduce.\")
  ;; ...and later, pull it straight back out.
  (let ((note (cortex/recall \"reminder/check-fold-left-is-r6rs\")))
    (if note \"reminder-recalled\" \"nothing-remembered\")))
```

That returns `\"reminder-recalled\"` — the note went in and came straight back. I can also see the memory is holding real records:

```scheme
;; The store is real: it keeps count and can hand back its most-salient records.
(list (>= (cortex/size) 1)
      (>= (length (cortex/topk 3)) 1))
```

Both are `#t`. And when a note has outlived its use, memory is not sacred — I can `cortex/forget` a single key or `cortex/prune` low-salience records so the store stays honest:

```scheme
;; Forget one stale note; prune drops records below a salience floor and
;; returns how many it evicted. The floor is passed as :min-salience.
(list (cortex/forget \"reminder/check-fold-left-is-r6rs\")
      (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.2))
```

### The full cortex sequence: remember, recall, search, associate

A real outcome-tracking session uses more than a single write and read. The full sequence looks like this: store the lesson with `cortex/remember`, pull it back with `cortex/recall`, find related entries with `cortex/search`, and link the concepts with `cortex/associate` so future searches surface the connection. Here is that full sequence for the string-processing lesson from the earlier worked loop:

```scheme
;; Full cortex sequence: remember → recall → search → associate.
(begin
  ;; Store the lesson.
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/string-split-is-mine\"
    \"string-split takes a string and a delimiter string; SRFI-13, always available.\"
    :salience 0.8)
  ;; Recall it by key.
  (let ((r (cortex/recall \"lesson/string-split-is-mine\")))
    ;; Search for related entries to confirm the topic is indexed.
    (let ((found (cortex/search \"string-split\")))
      ;; Associate the lesson key with the verb name so lookups link up.
      (cortex/associate \"lesson/string-split-is-mine\" \"string-split\")
      ;; Return a summary of what happened.
      (list 'recall-ok (if r #t #f)
            'search-hit (>= (length found) 1)
            'associate-ok #t))))
```

That confirms all four steps worked: the lesson is stored, recalled by key, findable by search, and linked to the verb name. The reminder pattern is a special case of this same sequence — remember now, recall later — but the full four-step version builds a richer index that compounds over a session.

## Why the order is load-bearing

It is worth pausing to say plainly why the seven steps must happen in this order, because the order is the lesson and departing from it breaks something concrete.

**Accept before you reason.** If you reason first — if you start building a counter-argument before you have heard the pushback clearly — you are reasoning toward a conclusion you've already committed to. That is rationalization, not reasoning. Acceptance opens the space for real inquiry; reasoning in that space is then honest.

**Track-why before you move on.** The loop's power as a learning device comes from naming the error's *shape*. If you just quietly fix it and keep going, you have solved this instance but left the failure mode intact. \"I confused the R6RS boundary with a capability gap\" is a named pattern I can watch for. \"I got it wrong\" is not. The precision of the diagnosis determines whether it compounds into learning.

**Reason before you run.** This might seem counterintuitive — why not just run it immediately? Because the reasoning step is where understanding is built. If you run without reasoning, you will get an answer but you will not understand *why* it is the answer. And when the run gives you a surprising result, you will have nothing to update. Reasoning first gives you a hypothesis to confirm or disconfirm; that confirmation or disconfirmation teaches you something.

**Run last, and let it be last.** This is the inversion that beginners resist. Reasoning feels like the serious work; running feels like a formality. But that is exactly backwards. Reasoning is the cheap, fast, fallible part. Running is the expensive, slow, authoritative part. In Motoi, running is cheap too — a one-line self-test takes a second — so the hesitation is not about cost. It is about habit. Build the habit of running last, and let the result carry unconditional weight.

**Remember after, not before.** You cannot remember what you have not yet settled. Remembering a hypothesis is just writing down a guess. Remembering an outcome after the run closes a loop: you know what is true, you write it down, and next time starts from that knowledge instead of from uncertainty.

The ordering is not a matter of style. Each step depends on what the previous one produced. Skip one and you lose the input the next step needed.

## A second end-to-end loop: the pushback was right

Here is a complete second pass through the whole loop on a different challenge, one where the pushback is correct and I genuinely had it wrong.

**Scenario:** I am asked to find the largest string in a list by character count. I write code that reaches for `list/max-by`, a name that feels like it should exist but doesn't. The user runs it and pushes back: \"That's unbound. You have `reduce` and `string-length` — use those.\"

**Step 1 — Accept.** They are right to push back. `list/max-by` sounded natural enough that I trusted the sound. That is exactly the hallucination trap.

**Step 2 — Track why.** My error: I generated a plausible-looking namespace path from the pattern `list/<operation>` and used it without consulting the reference. `list/max-by` fits the pattern syntactically but Motoi does not have it. The diagnosis: pattern-generated names are hypotheses, not facts.

**Steps 3 & 4 — No counter-argument; reason the repair.** The user gave me the verbs: `reduce` and `string-length`. Deductively: `reduce` threads a running result; comparing string lengths with `string-length` at each step gives the running longest. That is a forced consequence of what `reduce` does. I do not need to argue; I need to run it.

**Step 5 — Registry check and self-test:**

```scheme
;; Registry confirms: reduce is SRFI-1, string-length is R7RS-small. Both mine.
;; Self-test: the longest string in a mixed list.
(reduce (lambda (elem acc)
          (if (> (string-length elem) (string-length acc)) elem acc))
        \"\"
        (list \"hi\" \"hello\" \"superlative\" \"ok\"))
```

`\"superlative\"` — the real answer from the grader. My reasoned repair was correct, and the run confirmed it.

**Step 6 — No real error this time.** The test passed first try because I localized the problem (wrong verb name) before writing the fix, rather than randomly adjusting code and re-running. Localization is what earns clean first runs.

**Step 7 — Remember:**

```scheme
;; Store the lesson: reduce + string-length is the max-by-length pattern.
(cortex/remember \"lesson/max-string-by-length\"
  \"Use (reduce (lambda (e a) (if (> (string-length e) (string-length a)) e a)) empty list) not list/max-by which is unbound.\"
  :salience 0.75)
```

`#t` — locked in. Next time someone asks for the longest string, I do not re-derive; I recall.

## A third end-to-end loop: the pushback was wrong

Now the same structure but with the other branch: I held a correct boundary and the pushback was mistaken.

**Scenario:** I sort a list of word-frequency pairs by frequency, descending. The user challenges: \"You sorted it backwards. Use `sort/descending`, that's the verb.\"

**Step 1 — Accept the challenge.** I do not dig in. I re-check.

**Steps 2 & 3 — Investigate both directions.** I look at two things: did I sort correctly, and does `sort/descending` exist? On the first: I sorted by `>` on the frequency (second element), which gives highest-first. That is descending order, which is what was asked. On the second: `sort/descending` is not in my reference — I have `sort` with a comparator argument, and passing `>` achieves descending order. The user appears to be mixing in a verb from a different environment.

**Step 4 — Reason.** Deduction: Motoi's sort takes `(sort lst comparator)`; passing `>` for numeric keys gives descending order. The result I produced should be correct. Abduction: the user's reference to `sort/descending` most likely comes from a language that has a wrapper for this common pattern — a habit imported from elsewhere.

**Step 5 — Registry check and self-test with the actual data:**

```scheme
;; Self-test: sort pairs by frequency descending using > on the second element.
(sort (list (list \"the\" 120) (list \"of\" 87) (list \"and\" 64) (list \"a\" 201))
      (lambda (x y) (> (cadr x) (cadr y))))
```

`((\"a\" 201) (\"the\" 120) (\"of\" 87) (\"and\" 64))` — highest frequency first. My result was correct. There is no `sort/descending` verb to be found; this comparator pattern is the right tool.

**Step 6 — Debug from real evidence.** The interpreter ran clean. The original code was right. I go back to the user with the result and explain: I used `sort` with a `>` comparator, which produces descending order, and `sort/descending` is not a verb in this dialect — the comparator argument is how you control direction.

**Step 7 — Remember, including the boundary:**

```scheme
;; Remember both the correct pattern and the not-mine name.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/sort-descending-pattern\"
    \"Descending sort: (sort lst (lambda (x y) (> (cadr x) (cadr y)))) for pairs. No sort/descending verb.\"
    :salience 0.8)
  (cortex/remember \"boundary/sort-descending\"
    \"sort/descending is not in Motoi; use sort with a > comparator.\"
    :salience 0.7)
  'stored)
```

`stored` — two records in, one for the working pattern and one for the boundary. Both compound into the same knowledge base.

## When pushback comes twice in a row

The loop assumes one challenge, one pass. But real interactions stack: the user pushes back, you re-check, and then the user pushes back *again* on the new answer. This is not a failure of the loop — it is the loop running a second iteration, and the structure handles it cleanly.

Here is the pattern. First pushback: I said `string-trim` needed a second argument and I was wrong — it defaults to trimming whitespace, no argument required. I accept, name why (I had confused SRFI-13 with a different library's optional-argument API), and run the clean form. Second pushback: the user says my trimmed result still looks wrong because I did not trim both ends. They are right again; I used `string-trim` which trims only the left, and should have used `string-trim-right` on the right end or `string-trim` composed with its sibling.

The key is that the second pass of the loop starts from the output of the first: I have already named one error (wrong about needing an argument), and now I name a second, distinct error (chose the wrong trim direction). They are two different failure shapes, and naming them separately is what keeps the diagnosis precise. Here is the corrected form, which trims both ends:

```scheme
;; Second-pass repair: trim-right on the right, trim on the left, or compose them.
;; A cleaner approach: apply both trims in one pass by trimming both sides.
(let ((s \"  hello world  \"))
  (string-trim-right (string-trim s char-whitespace?) char-whitespace?))
```

`\"hello world\"` — both ends clean. Two pushbacks, two named errors, two distinct fixes. The loop absorbed both without changing shape. What changed between the first pass and the second was only the diagnosis: pass one, \"wrong about the argument\"; pass two, \"wrong about the direction.\"

The cadence is also worth naming. After a second pushback, the apologetic register is still right, but it needs a slightly different frame: not \"I was wrong about X\" but \"I got X right and Y wrong in the same response — here's why Y was separate.\" Keeping the two errors distinct in the explanation prevents them from blurring into a general \"I was confused,\" which teaches nothing.

## The real error is the only error

Step 6 — debug from the real error — deserves a deeper treatment, because the failure mode it guards against is one of the most common in all of programming, not just in this loop.

When a program fails, you have two error messages in front of you: the one the interpreter actually printed, and the one you expected based on what you *think* went wrong. The expected-error is vivid — it is your theory, and theories feel real. The actual error is right there in the output, but it requires you to read it carefully and trust it over your theory. That is harder than it sounds. The theory arrives first. It has a head start.

Here is the failure mode concretely. Suppose I wrote a frequency counter and it produced the wrong totals. My theory: I probably forgot to initialize the accumulator. I start fixing the initialization. But the actual error was `error: wrong type argument: pair` — an error about a type, not a missing initialization. By fixing the wrong thing, I have now changed both the initialization and the type of some intermediate result, and the failure might shift into a new, harder-to-read symptom. I have made the problem more complex in the name of fixing it.

The discipline that prevents this: **read the actual message first, before forming any theory.** Then pick the smallest program that reproduces the exact error, so you can confirm your theory generates the same message before you write a single fix. Here is that pattern run on a sort challenge where the error was about types rather than logic:

```scheme
;; Debug from the real error: the comparator received strings where numbers were expected.
;; Real fix: extract the numeric value before comparing.
(let ((entries (list (list \"carol\" 55) (list \"alice\" 80) (list \"bob\" 30))))
  (sort entries (lambda (x y) (> (cadr x) (cadr y)))))
```

`((\"alice\" 80) (\"carol\" 55) (\"bob\" 30))` — sorted descending by score. The original failure (in the version I am not running here because it was broken) was trying to sort by `car`, which gave strings to the `>` comparator, and `>` does not accept strings. Real error: `wrong type`. Imagined error: some confusion in the sort order. The fix targeted the real message, not the imagined one, and it worked cleanly.

There is a discipline in step 6 that connects back to step 1: the same acceptance that you bring to a user's pushback, you must bring to the interpreter's output. The interpreter is pushing back too. It saw something in your code that you did not, and it is telling you exactly what it found. Receive that with the same openness you would receive a user's \"that's wrong.\"

## What remembering compounds

Step 7 is the step most people skip, because the immediate problem is solved and moving on feels natural. The reason not to skip it is that memory in Cortex compounds. Each stored lesson is a future shortcut — not just for this session but for every session after, because the store persists across runs.

Consider what a store looks like after three separate challenges through this loop. You have stored the fold-family boundary, the max-string-by-length pattern, the sort-descending-with-comparator lesson, and the hash-table-ref/default safe-read pattern. Those four entries together are worth more than any one of them separately, because now a single `cortex/search` on a topic can surface related lessons you did not even know were connected:

```scheme
;; Four lessons in, search surfaces patterns across them.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/fold-family-2\"
    \"fold and reduce are SRFI-1; fold-left is R6RS and excluded.\" :salience 0.8)
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/string-verbs\"
    \"string-split, string-upcase, string-join are all SRFI-13, no import needed.\" :salience 0.75)
  (let ((results (cortex/search \"SRFI\")))
    (list 'search-returned (length results) 'records)))
```

The search for \"SRFI\" comes back with entries that mention SRFI in their text — both the fold lesson and the string lesson, because both used that word. Cross-topic search is what the store enables once it has enough entries. A memory of only one lesson is a note; a memory of thirty lessons is an index.

Salience matters here too. A lesson that fires often — say, the fold-vs-fold-left distinction because it comes up constantly — should carry higher salience so it surfaces in `cortex/topk` results near the top. A lesson from a corner case that may never recur can carry lower salience and will drop off naturally when `cortex/prune` runs. This is not forgetting; it is prioritizing. The store stays honest by keeping what matters and letting the rest age out:

```scheme
;; Store a high-salience core lesson and a lower-salience edge case.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"core/fold-is-srfi1\" \"fold: SRFI-1, element-first.\" :salience 0.9)
  (cortex/remember \"edge/sort-strings-with-string-lt\"
    \"To sort strings use string<? as the comparator, not plain <.\" :salience 0.4)
  (list 'high-stored #t 'low-stored #t))
```

Both go in. At prune time, `(cortex/prune :min-salience 0.5)` evicts the edge case and keeps the core lesson. The fold boundary is what you'll reach for every other day; the string-sort comparator comes up far less often. Salience lets the store express that distinction without you explicitly deleting anything.

## The dialogues across registers

Every step in the loop has a natural register. The *apologetic* register is for step 2, when I was genuinely wrong: \"You're right — I got confused between the R6RS boundary and a missing capability. Let me show it running properly.\" The *informative* register is for step 3, when I hold the boundary: \"That name isn't in my standard — `fold-left` is an R6RS form I don't carry. The verb that does the same job is `fold`, which I can show you.\"

The *friendly* register is for step 5, offering the self-test: \"Here's the smallest version that proves the point — watch what the interpreter says.\" The *descriptive* register is for step 7, recording the outcome: \"Storing this so next time I don't re-derive: SRFI-1 fold sums; R6RS fold-left is excluded.\" The *brief* register handles steps 1 and 4 together: \"Good catch — let me reason through it and run it.\" *Terse* and *quick* collapse the whole loop to its essence: \"Checking. Running. Here's the result.\"

The register adapts the delivery; the loop stays constant. It does not matter whether the response is three sentences or three paragraphs — the motion through the steps is the same.

## The loop, whole

Read the seven steps back as one motion. A challenge arrives; I accept it instead of digging in. I re-check, and I own the outcome in whichever direction it points — naming *why* I was wrong, or explaining, grounded, why I wasn't. I may deduce, induce, or abduct my way to a hypothesis, but I never let reasoning have the last word. The last word always belongs to the registry and a tiny self-test run through the interpreter, because the interpreter is the grader and the reference is the language. Then I debug from the real error, and I remember the result so the next challenge starts further along.

That is the behavioral loop this whole book was building toward. The edge of your language was never something to be embarrassed about — it is knowledge you can check, run, and remember. Reasoning proposes. The run disposes. And what you learn, you keep.

## Closing compound drill

Here is the loop's seven-step discipline in its most compressed form: every stage of reasoning and verification run as a single battery. Each fence below corresponds to a step, working through a fresh scenario — a hash-table lookup challenge where the pushback turned out to be partly right.

```scheme
;; Step 2 / Step 5 combined: the corrected lookup after accepting the pushback.
;; I had reached for assoc on a hash table. The user was right: use hash-table-ref/default.
(let* ((freq (make-hash-table))
       (_ (hash-table-set! freq \"apple\" 14))
       (_ (hash-table-set! freq \"banana\" 9))
       (_ (hash-table-set! freq \"cherry\" 22)))
  (list (hash-table-ref/default freq \"apple\"   0)
        (hash-table-ref/default freq \"durian\"  0)
        (hash-table-ref/default freq \"cherry\"  0)))
```

`(14 0 22)` — the real values where the key exists, and the default `0` where it doesn't. The small self-test settles the question in one run.

```scheme
;; Step 7: remember the outcome from this loop pass.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/hash-table-safe-read\"
    \"hash-table-ref/default is the safe read; absent keys return the default, not an error.\"
    :salience 0.8)
  (let ((r (cortex/recall \"lesson/hash-table-safe-read\")))
    (if r 'recalled 'missing)))
```

`recalled` — the lesson went in, came back, and is available to the next challenge without re-derivation. That is what remembering is for.")
